{"name":"Sylph of Light","hex":"#f7fb4e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sylph","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An invisible being of the air.","The elemental being of air, usually female."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sylph"},{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sylph","blocks":[{"text":"A sylph is an air spirit stemming from the 16th-century works of Paracelsus, who describes sylphs as (invisible) beings of the air, his elementals of air. A significant number of subsequent literary and occult works have been inspired by Paracelsus's concept: Robert Alfred Vaughan noted that \"the wild but poetical fantasies\" of Paracelsus had probably exercised a larger influence over his age and the subsequent one than is generally supposed, particularly on the Rosicrucians, but that through the 18th century they had become reduced to \"machinery for the playwright\" and \"opera figurantes with wings of gauze and spangles\"."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Praetorius%281666%29-p0000-11Luftleute%26Windmenschen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sylph"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Paracelsus.jpg/500px-Paracelsus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Paracelsus.jpg/1280px-Paracelsus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Paracelsus.jpg/500px-Paracelsus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Swiss alchemist and philosopher Theophrastus von Hohenheim (\"Paracelsus\") first created the concept of the sylph.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParacelsus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg/500px-La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg/1280px-La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg/500px-La_Sylphide_by_A.M._Bininger%2C_bourbon_whiskey_label%2C_ca._1860.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"La Sylphide Bourbon, A.M. Bininger & Co. Bourbon adverti